.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt DTRACE_FDOPEN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtrace_fdopen
.Nd create a DTrace handle from a delegated consumer descriptor
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libdtrace
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtrace.h
.Ft dtrace_hdl_t *
.Fn dtrace_fdopen "int fd" "int version" "int flags" "int *errp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn dtrace_fdopen
function is the capability-safe counterpart of
.Fn dtrace_open .
It initializes a DTrace handle using an already-open DTrace consumer
descriptor instead of opening
.Pa /dev/dtrace/dtrace .
It does not inspect provider device paths, load kernel modules, or open the
fasttrap device.
.Pp
The supplied descriptor is duplicated with close-on-exec set.
The caller retains ownership of
.Fa fd
on both success and failure.
The returned handle owns the duplicate, which is released by
.Fn dtrace_close .
.Pp
.Dv DTRACE_O_NODEV
and vector operation are incompatible with a supplied consumer descriptor.
Provider modules needed by the tracing program must already be loaded.
Fasttrap-based providers are unavailable unless a future delegated-session
interface supplies their separately constrained resources.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn dtrace_fdopen
returns a DTrace handle.
On failure it returns
.Dv NULL
and, when
.Fa errp
is non-null, stores either an errno value or a libdtrace error code there.
.Sh ERRORS
In addition to errors reported by
.Fn dtrace_open ,
.Fn dtrace_fdopen
fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a negative descriptor or incompatible flags, and with errors from
.Xr fcntl 2
or DTrace ioctls when the descriptor is unusable.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr dtrace 1
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Descriptor rights and ioctl restrictions limit operations on the DTrace
consumer but do not express probe, target, predicate, or D action policy.
Delegation must therefore remain an explicit administrator decision.
